{"attributes": {"type": "DifferentialExperiment", "particle": "decay", "reaction": "PFNS", "nuclide": "Cf-252", "library": "ARIADNE", "documentation": "EXFOR No. = 30969.002. Journal article = Nuclear Science and Engineering,Vol.106, p.353 (1990), USA. The data by Maerten et al. are in EXFOR sub-entry 30969.002 (measured at 0 degree) and 30969.003 (measured at 60 degree)were published as a jornal publication~\\cite{Maerten1990} contrary to EXFOR sub-entries 31854.002 and 31854.003 by the same group that were previously published as laboratory reports and proceeding~\\cite{Maerten1984, Maerten1983, Maerten1982a, Maerten1982b, Maerten1982c}. The 30969.002 data were finalized after Mannhart's evaluation, but were recommended by him to be taken into account. This is the 0 degree measurement, which is strongly correlated with the 60 degree measurement. Maerten et al. recorded neutron spectra as a function of TOF and sctillator pulse height; PSD corrected for part of the background (from alphas, $\\gamma$s, and random coincidences). The team used two neutron detectors of type NE213 at two different angles and TOF paths: (a) A 12.7-cm thick and 12.7-cm diameter scintillator was used at 0 degree with a TOF path length of 5.905 m and a time resolution of 1.5 ns. (b) A 3.81-cm thick and 12.7-cm diameter scintillator was used at 60 degree with a TOF path length of 3.275 m  and a time resolution of 1.3 ns. The neutron-detector efficiency (undocumented) is simulated via a Monte Carlo by Stanton~\\cite{Stanton:1971} using light output curves by~\\cite{Cecil:1979}. The authors carefully adjusted the simulation to consider the detector signal resolution (light output in terms of proton recoil equivalent). Also, they absolutely calibrated neutron detection efficiencies of both detectors against their response on 14-MeV neutrons from a DT neutron generator. The neutron source strength has been determined by counting the associated alpha-particles produced in the DT reaction (solid state detector positioned at a fixed backward angle)~\\cite{Maerten:2023privcom}.   The detector response accounts for multiple scattering and attenuation in air, the surrounding, and detector walls. Fission fragments were detected as stop signal (delayed by 200 ns) in an 99.2$\\pm$0.2\\% efficient minitiuarized fission chamber. The $^{252}$Cf deposit had an activity of 70000 f/s. The fitted Maxwellian to these data had a temperature of 1.374 MeV.\nCorrections were applied for nearly all effects mentioned above; it is only unknown if the decay of the sample was corrected for. Additionally,the following corrections are listed in the journal article: gamma peak correction (negligible), TOF channel dependent background due to non-correlated stop signals (negligible), unfolding correction due to time resolution, bin-width correction, and fragment detection efficiency. Statistical and systematic uncertainties are provided in the EXFOR entry 30969.002 based on Table~II of Ref.~\\cite{Maerten1990}. The systematic uncertainties contain uncertainties of the neutron detection efficiency, energy scale, energy bin width, light-output calibration and all corrections. Reference~\\cite{Maerten1990} cites to \\cite{Maerten1984} for some partial uncertainties. Hence, we take from 31854.002 partial uncertainties for bin width correction, energy scale,  unfolding correction due to time resolution, multiple-scattering correction, light-output bias, and neutron detector efficiency, and back out unaccounted for systematic uncertainties. Impurity uncertainties were not provided, and were assumed from templates, but are small. One major concern of entry 31854.002 (previous data~\\cite{Maerten1984}) was that the neutron-detecor efficiency was derived in a circular fashion by using the NBS $^{252}$Cf spectrum as input for validating the detector response. This was corrected here~\\cite{Maerten1990} by undertaking measurements of the detector response wit a DT generator that was also measured with the associated particle technique~\\cite{Maerten:2023privcom}. Given that and the thoughtful consideration of all necessary corrections, we accept data set 30969.002~\\cite{Maerten1990} for the new standard evaluation and reject previous data~\\cite{Maerten1984}. We also reject data above 20 MeV from~\\cite{Maerten1990}; the data rely on extrapolating the detector response beyond the DT measurement and on simulations using carbon cross sections that changed significantly above 15 MeV since the mid-eighties. Not surprisingly, the data above 20 MeV show an unexpected upturn.", "date": "2023-07-05", "Author": ["Maerten", "Richter", "Seeliger", "Fromm", "Boettger", "Klein"], "Year": "1990", "Facility": "N/A", "Institute": ["3DDRTUD", "3DDRROS", "2GERPTB"], "Isotope": "Cf-252", "Quantity": "PFNS", "MeasurementType": "PFNS", "ConcernEXFOR": "None", "ConcernAIACHNE": ["DetectorEfficiency (above 15 MeV)"], "ConcernAIACHNE_text": "The detector efficiency above 15 MeV isextrapolated. Data are cut off at 20 MeV.", "BackgroundCorrected": "y", "AlphaBackground": "y", "GammaBackground": "y", "RandomCoincidenceBackground": "y", "ShadowBarBackground": "N/A", "WrapAroundBackground": "y", "MultipleScatteringSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "MultipleScatteringSurroundingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSurroundingCorrected": "y", "FissionDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionResponseCorrected": "y", "SampleDecayCorrected": "unknown", "FissionFragmentAbsorptioninSampleCorrected": "y", "SignalPulsePileupCorrected": "y", "DeadtimeCorrected": "y", "AngularDistributionFissionFragmentsCorrected": "y", "ImpuritiesCorrected": "y", "Mannhart": "after evaluation", "CorrelatedData": ["31854.002", "31854.003", "30969.003"], "EXFORnumber": "30969.002", "Accepted": "y", "MissingCorrections": ["SampleDecay"], "FissionDetector": {"Type": "IOCH", "ParticleDetected": "FF", "Efficiency": 99.2, "EfficiencyMe